  {
    type: "gate",
    id: "g2",
    kind: "number",
    prompt: `Working on $${printEq(WE)}$: taking logs and dividing gives $${idx(WE.m, WE.c)} = \\frac{\\log ${WE.R}}{\\log ${WE.L}}$. What is $x$, correct to two decimal places?`,
    answer: d2(WE.x).toFixed(2),
    explain: `$\\frac{\\log ${WE.R}}{\\log ${WE.L}} = ${d6(L10(WE.R) / L10(WE.L))}$, so $${WE.m}x = ${d6(L10(WE.R) / L10(WE.L) + 2)}$ and $x = ${d2(WE.x).toFixed(2)}$.`,
  },
  { type: "h", text: "2. The brackets are the marks" },
  {
    type: "p",
    md: `The index is a whole expression, so all of it comes down together and all of it needs a bracket: $(${idx(EXA.m, EXA.c)}) \\log ${EXA.L}$, never $${EXA.m}x ${EXA.c < 0 ? "-" : "+"} ${Math.abs(EXA.c)} \\log ${EXA.L}$.\nWithout the bracket the logarithm multiplies only the last term, and the equation being solved is no longer the one that was set. It is the single most reported slip on this topic.`,
